# Deploy a Router

This notebook installs a tuned artifact in a small OTP routing service. You
start a `GenServer` with a baseline router, route one request, tune the
router, and install the saved artifact while the service runs. Then you try an
artifact learned for a different router and see that the service keeps the
last valid program. The router is plain Elixir code tuned with the `:grid`
strategy, so the notebook runs offline with no credentials and no model calls.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Define the router

The router sends a request to the review queue when its risk score reaches
the threshold. `new/0` returns fresh router code with the baseline threshold.

```elixir
defmodule RiskRouter do
  @moduledoc "Routes requests by risk score."

  defstruct threshold: 0.9, review_queue: "risk-review", standard_queue: "general"

  def new, do: %__MODULE__{}

  def route(router, %{risk: risk}) do
    if risk >= router.threshold, do: router.review_queue, else: router.standard_queue
  end
end
```

## Define the service

The service state is the current router and a `Task.Supervisor`. Each request
asks the server for a snapshot of the current router and runs it in a
supervised task, outside the server process. A slow request does not block
installs, and an install does not change a request that already holds its
snapshot.

`install/2` takes artifact JSON. It decodes the artifact with
`Tune.Artifact.decode/2` and applies it to fresh router code with
`Tune.load/2`. The server replaces its router only when both steps succeed.

```elixir
defmodule RouterService do
  @moduledoc "Serves the current router and installs validated artifacts."

  use GenServer

  def start_link(opts), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, opts)

  def install(server, json), do: GenServer.call(server, {:install, json})

  def route(server, request, timeout \\ 1_000) do
    {router, tasks} = GenServer.call(server, :snapshot)
    task = Task.Supervisor.async_nolink(tasks, fn -> RiskRouter.route(router, request) end)

    case Task.yield(task, timeout) || Task.shutdown(task, :brutal_kill) do
      {:ok, queue} -> {:ok, queue}
      {:exit, reason} -> {:error, reason}
      nil -> {:error, :timeout}
    end
  end

  @impl true
  def init(opts), do: {:ok, {RiskRouter.new(), Keyword.fetch!(opts, :tasks)}}

  @impl true
  def handle_call(:snapshot, _from, state), do: {:reply, state, state}

  def handle_call({:install, json}, _from, {_router, tasks} = state) do
    with {:ok, artifact} <- Tune.Artifact.decode(json),
         {:ok, router} <- Tune.load(artifact, RiskRouter.new()) do
      {:reply, :ok, {router, tasks}}
    else
      {:error, _error} = error -> {:reply, error, state}
    end
  end
end
```

## Route with the baseline

Start the task supervisor and the service, then route a request with risk
`0.7`.

```elixir
{:ok, tasks} = Task.Supervisor.start_link()
{:ok, service} = RouterService.start_link(tasks: tasks)

RouterService.route(service, %{risk: 0.7})
```

```text
{:ok, "general"}
```

The baseline threshold is `0.9`, so the request goes to the standard queue.

## Tune and save the router

Search three thresholds on validation data. The `:grid` strategy changes the
`threshold` field of the router struct and keeps the other fields fixed.

```elixir
routing = fn tune_case, queue -> if queue == tune_case.expected, do: 1, else: 0 end

{:ok, winner} =
  Tune.new(RiskRouter.new(), run: &RiskRouter.route/2)
  |> Tune.data(
    validation: [
      %{id: "low-risk", input: %{risk: 0.2}, expected: "general"},
      %{id: "elevated-risk", input: %{risk: 0.7}, expected: "risk-review"}
    ]
  )
  |> Tune.vary(threshold: [0.9, 0.75, 0.6])
  |> Tune.score(:routing, routing)
  |> Tune.search(:grid)
  |> Tune.tune()

{:ok, artifact} = Tune.dump(winner)
{:ok, json} = Tune.Artifact.encode(artifact)

{artifact.parameters, artifact.provenance["kind"]}
```

```text
{%{"threshold" => 0.6}, "generic"}
```

`Tune.dump/1` builds a `Tune.Artifact` that holds only the learned values,
a fingerprint of their contract, and safe provenance. `Tune.Artifact.encode/1`
turns it into canonical JSON that you can store and ship with a release.

## Install the artifact

Install the JSON in the running service and repeat the request.

```elixir
:ok = RouterService.install(service, json)

RouterService.route(service, %{risk: 0.7})
```

```text
{:ok, "risk-review"}
```

The service decoded the artifact, applied the threshold to a fresh
`RiskRouter`, and now sends the request to review.

## Reject an incompatible artifact

Another team tuned an older router that stores the threshold as an integer
percentage. Its artifact has the same parameter name but a different value
contract.

```elixir
{:ok, legacy_winner} =
  Tune.new(%{threshold: 90},
    run: fn router, %{risk: risk} ->
      if risk * 100 >= router.threshold, do: "risk-review", else: "general"
    end
  )
  |> Tune.data(validation: [%{id: "elevated-risk", input: %{risk: 0.7}, expected: "risk-review"}])
  |> Tune.vary(threshold: [90, 60])
  |> Tune.score(:routing, routing)
  |> Tune.search(:grid)
  |> Tune.tune()

{:ok, legacy_artifact} = Tune.dump(legacy_winner)
{:ok, legacy_json} = Tune.Artifact.encode(legacy_artifact)

{:error, error} = RouterService.install(service, legacy_json)
Exception.message(error)
```

```text
"artifact parameters do not match the fresh target"
```

`Tune.load/2` compares the artifact fingerprint with the fresh router before
it changes anything. The install fails, and the service keeps serving the
last valid router.

```elixir
RouterService.route(service, %{risk: 0.7})
```

```text
{:ok, "risk-review"}
```

An artifact document with missing fields fails the same way: `Tune.Artifact.decode/2` returns an error,
and the server state stays unchanged.

```elixir
{:error, error} = RouterService.install(service, ~s({"version": 1}))
{Exception.message(error), RouterService.route(service, %{risk: 0.7})}
```

```text
{"artifact has unknown or missing fields", {:ok, "risk-review"}}
```

## Stop the service

```elixir
GenServer.stop(service)
Supervisor.stop(tasks)
```

The service keeps its router in memory. Durable storage, rollout across
nodes, and when to install a new artifact are host decisions.

